  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nIt is going to be a warm weekend, likely the last of the year, \nwith widespread 80s on deck both Saturday and Sunday. We are \nalready starting to see an increased low-level response to the \ntroughing over western North America. For us, that means \nincreasing southeasterly winds and WAA. By tomorrow, the surface\nlow will move east out of the Canadian Rockies, placing the \nUpper MS Valley within the warm sector. Strong southerly winds \nwill develop by the afternoon, gusting between 30 to 40 mph. \nAreas along the Buffalo Ridge could even see gusts exceed 40 mph\nduring peak mixing hours. A Wind Advisory was considered, \nhowever, current forecast gusts fall just short of the lower end\nof the criteria. If trends increase, an Advisory may be needed.\n\nThe other thing to watch for this weekend will be the near-\nrecord temperatures and elevated fire weather conditions. The \nlatest run of the NBM has trended slightly higher for \ntemperatures on both Saturday and Sunday, with the 50th \npercentile showing widespread mid- upper 80s each day. There is \ngreater uncertainty with Sunday's highs, but this upward trend \nwould lean things towards a later fropa Sunday night. The \nrecords for our three climate sites may actually have an easier \ntime being beaten on Sunday rather than Saturday, despite the \nhigher confidence in higher temps for tomorrow. This is because \nthe records for October 11th are about 5 degrees lower than \nOctober 10th. For the 10th, the records are: MSP (90 in 1928), \nSTC (88 in 1928), and EAU (88 in 1928). For the 11th, the \nrecords are: MSP (85 in 2015), STC (83 in 2015), and EAU (85 in\n1930).\n\nThese potentially record-breaking temps will result in elevated\nfire weather conditions for at least Saturday and possibly on \nSunday as well. The strong winds will be there for Saturday, but\nweaken as we get into Sunday. For both days, RH values will \ndrop into the 30s during the afternoon. Typically we start to \nworry most when RHs drop below 25%, so the more we can limit how\ndry we get, the better. Given our wet start to the fall, the \namount of dry fuels (dead vegetation) is not as concerning as it\nmight typically be for this time of year. Taking both of those \naspects into account, and after coordinating with the DNR, we \nwill continue to lean against issuing any Red Flag Warnings for \nthis weekend. If RH values trend lower than expected, that is \nwhat would push us towards issuing a Warning. Otherwise, what is\nmore likely is that we issue a Special Weather Statement for \nthe elevated to near-critical fire weather conditions, \nespecially across western Minnesota.\n\nNEXT WEEK... Temperatures trend down following the cold front  \npassage Sunday into Monday, with much of the week seeing \ntemperatures closer to normal for this time of year. While there\nmay be light precipitation along the fropa early in the week, \nthe best chance for rain will come mid-week, likely on \nThursday. The forcing is much more impressive with this wave, \nwith long range models suggesting we even build in a few hundred\nJoules of CAPE.",
